The kind of the field with the keyboard is known only once its client has said it (Istesso's
ADR 0015, "文字の入力の欄"; ADR 0007, "変換中の文字"). Trial only.

Seen on the host (Chrome 145 with the trial's switches and environment, its Wayland messages
written out): when the focus moves to a field, by a click or by Tab, Chrome sends `disable`,
`commit`, `enable`, `commit` and nothing else, whatever the field is. It holds back the content
type, the cursor rectangle and the surrounding text until the compositor has acknowledged that
commit with `done`, which this compositor (it has no input method) sent only with text. So an
enabled text input that had said nothing was taken for a normal field: Chrome's password field was
told to the device as a field like any other (state 1), the first composing text went into it,
and only that made Chrome say "password" (after a click; after Tab often not even then).
Firefox and the Text Editor (GTK 3 and 4) say the content type together with `enable`.

- A text input that is enabled and has not said a content type since is a field of unknown
  kind. It is told to the device as a password field (state 2: no new value, nothing changes
  on the wire), takes no composing text, and a copy from it is refused, until its client says
  what it is.
- The client is asked: (1) the commit that enabled it is acknowledged with `done` (nothing
  goes before it, so it changes nothing in the field); Chrome then says its content type,
  rectangle and surrounding text at once. (2) If it answers without a content type (Chrome
  after Tab, now and then: the rectangle alone), or not within 40 ms, its text input is told
  that it left the surface and entered it again, once; the keyboard is not touched, and the
  page sees neither a blur nor a focus (seen). It enables itself again, is acknowledged again,
  and says the content type (seen, 8 of 8). (3) If it still says none, it is left at that: a
  client that has said a content type at some time stays a password field for the device; one
  that has never said any gets the protocol's default, a normal field.
- While the client is being asked, nothing is told; what comes of it is told at once. Seen with
  Chrome, 201 moves by click and Tab: the right state 4 ms after the move in the middle, 9 ms at
  the longest (step 2 was needed 8 times, step 3 never).
- A client that says the content type with `enable` is never asked, and nothing changes for it.
- The field's serial goes up with every enabling of a text input that was not enabled before
  (not for the answer to this side's own leave and enter): a client with one text input for
  all its fields (Chrome, Firefox) disables and enables it when the focus moves among them,
  faster than "not in a field" is told in between, so the serial stayed the same and the
  device went on composing into the next field. Firefox says `enable` once more for the field
  it is in, with its next commit, in the middle of composing (seen): that is the same field.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of every other patch in this directory, in the order of
their names (its name sorts last on purpose). FOR TRIALS AND EVERYDAY USE ONLY; not for main
before a review (it touches the input path).

